<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page1-what-is-a-game" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>What is an Extensive Form Game?</title>

  <p>
    This is our first example of a <em>game</em> in the mathematical sense: two
    people, each trying to do the best for themselves, where what's "best"
    depends on what the other person does. It looks like a simple story about
    a fender-bender, but the way we turn it into a diagram &#8212; a
    <term>game tree</term> &#8212; is a structure we will use for the rest of
    the semester. Pay close attention to how the story becomes a picture.
  </p>

  <task xml:id="read-1-1">
    <title>Reading: Section 1.1, Tony's Accident</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Section 1.1 of <em>Game Theory in Action</em>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="reflect-1-1" type="reflection">
    <title>Pause and Reflect</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Before you read on in the book: if you were advising Tony after the
        first game tree (Figure 1.1), what would you tell him to do?
      </p>
      <p>
        Now that you've seen how the book resolves it &#8212; Tony is out
        $80 no matter what he does &#8212; does that match your instinct?
        The authors write, "We did not like this game." What do you think is
        bugging them about it, if anything is bugging you too?
      </p>
      <p>
        Write a few sentences. There's no single right answer here &#8212;
        this is about noticing your own intuition before the math takes
        over.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task xml:id="read-1-2">
    <title>Reading: Section 1.2, Games in Extensive Form with Complete Information</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Read Section 1.2. This section introduces the vocabulary we'll use
        to talk about game trees precisely &#8212; it's worth reading
        slowly. The interactive diagram and vocabulary list below cover the
        same ground and can help solidify what you just read.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <p>
    Below is the second game tree from the book (Figure 1.2), where Tony has
    a third option: send $40 up front and the rest once Vic provides a
    receipt. Use the buttons to explore what the different pieces of the
    tree are called.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-tree-toggle-1-1">
    <caption>
      An interactive version of Figure 1.2. Toggle between plain view, node
      types, a sample path through the tree, and the payoffs at each
      terminal node.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-tree-toggle-1-1"
      iframe="ch1-graph/tree-toggle-1-1.html" width="100%" aspect="19:10">
      <static>
        <p>
          A game tree for Tony's Accident with three of Tony's actions at
          the root (send $80, demand receipt, send $40), Vic responding
          with repair/don't repair after "demand receipt" and after "send
          $40," and payoffs (&#8722;80, 80), (&#8722;80, 20), (0, 0),
          (&#8722;80, 20), (&#8722;40, 40) at the five terminal nodes, in
          that order.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Now work through the vocabulary below. Each term has a quick
    self-check &#8212; if one surprises you, go back and look at the
    diagram above again before moving on.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-vocab-accordion-1-2">
    <caption>Key vocabulary from Section 1.2, with self-checks.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-vocab-accordion-1-2"
      iframe="ch1-graph/vocab-accordion-1-2.html" width="100%" aspect="4:3">
      <static>
        <p>
          A list of five terms with definitions and self-check questions:
          decision node, terminal node, action, payoff, and extensive form
          game.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

</section>
